{
  "teamId": 111,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 Boston Red Sox opened the season with a jolt of drama, swinging wildly between elation and heartbreak in their very first week. A damaging double off Garrett Whitlock on April 6th threatened to define the tone, but Romy Gonzalez answered immediately with a clutch double of his own to keep Boston afloat. That push-and-pull energy would become the signature of the Red Sox's spring, as Whitlock surrendered costly home runs and the bullpen proved unreliable, yet the offense kept finding ways to claw back into games.\n\nMay proved to be a crucible for Boston. A brutal stretch centered on May 13th saw Javier B\u00e1ez torch the bullpen twice in the same game \u2014 Greg Weissert's catastrophic outing producing a -0.643 WPA swing, the single worst moment of the entire regular season. Yet even amid the carnage, Kristian Campbell answered with a two-run home run that hinted at the kind of resilience this roster possessed. The Red Sox staggered through the month battered but alive, their pitching staff a genuine concern.\n\nThe summer turned Boston's fortunes. A remarkable Ceddanne Rafaela home run on July 11th \u2014 worth an extraordinary 0.821 WPA \u2014 ignited the team's best stretch of the year, and Romy Gonzalez's grand slam on July 23rd symbolized the offense hitting its stride. Nathaniel Lowe contributed timely power, and Rafaela kept delivering in August, his back-to-back clutch home runs making him the team's most electrifying performer. The Red Sox rode this wave to an 89-win season and an unlikely Wild Card berth, holding off challengers through September on the strength of late-inning grit and depth.\n\nOctober brought the Yankees in the Wild Card round, and for one fleeting moment it felt like Boston might pull off something special. Masataka Yoshida delivered a key two-run single in Game 1, and Aroldis Chapman steadied a shaky ninth. But Game 2 unraveled \u2014 a Ben Rice home run off Brayan Bello stung, and Austin Wells's single off Whitlock in a late inning proved to be the decisive blow. Trevor Story homered heroically, but the comeback fell short. The 2025 Red Sox were a good team that ran into a great one at the worst possible moment, ending a promising season one round too soon.",
